I've implemented the fix in `src/IO/parseDateTimeBestEffort.cpp`.

## What I changed

In `parseDateTimeBestEffortImpl`, in the non-strict/best-effort branch (line 785), I added a range check immediately after `adjust_time_zone` is applied to the UTC timestamp (line 790). The check only applies when `is_64` is `false` (i.e., parsing into a 32-bit `DateTime`):

```cpp
res = utc_time_zone.makeDateTime(year, month, day_of_month, hour, minute, second);
adjust_time_zone();

/// For usual DateTime check if value is within supported range after the timezone offset
/// is applied. Otherwise signal failure so the caller can fall back to DateTime64 instead
/// of wrapping around or clamping.
if constexpr (!is_64)
{
    if (res < 0 || res > UINT32_MAX)
        return on_error(ErrorCodes::CANNOT_PARSE_DATETIME, "Cannot read DateTime: value is out of range");
}
```

## Why this works

- The offset adjustment happens first, then the range check operates on the **UTC-adjusted** timestamp — so `'2106-02-07 06:28:15-01:00'` (which shifts forward past `UINT32_MAX`) and `'1970-01-01 00:00:00+01:00'` (which shifts before the epoch) both fail the `DateTime` path and fall back to `Nullable(DateTime64(9))` with the correct UTC value.
- Values that stay within `[0, UINT32_MAX]` after adjustment (e.g. `'2106-02-07 06:28:15+01:00'`) pass the check and remain `Nullable(DateTime)`.
- I used the existing `on_error` helper rather than a bare `return false`, because this branch is instantiated with `ReturnType = void` as well as `bool`. `on_error` returns `false` for the `bool` instantiation (triggering the `DateTime64` fallback in schema inference) and throws for the `void` instantiation — matching the surrounding convention and the identical check already present in the strict path (lines 818-822).

